        "Many schools solve the overlap sensibly: a student's IB Group 1 language and literature requirement is satisfied through Turkish A: Language and Literature, which is taught to IB's comparative, command-term standard while also counting toward the MEB Turkish-language requirement. Where a school runs the arrangement this way, a Turkish A tutor is effectively doing double duty, and sessions are planned with both marking schemes in view rather than just the IB syllabus guide.",
        "For MYP students the collision shows up earlier and less visibly, because MYP's eight subject groups and MEB's own middle-years requirements run on separate timetables inside the same school day. A student can be doing well against MYP criteria while still falling behind on MEB coursework that a report card tracks separately, or the reverse. Tutors who only look at the IB side of a Turkish MYP student's workload are missing half the picture, which is why we ask about both when a family first gets in touch.",
        "None of this is a criticism of the schools; it reflects that IB and Cambridge programmes operate in Türkiye under Ministry oversight rather than instead of it, and every authorised school has agreed to deliver both. What it means practically for a family is that a Turkish DP student's week is genuinely fuller than the IB subject guide alone suggests, and scheduling tutoring around exam periods needs to account for MEB assessment dates as well as IB deadlines.",
        "Families who are not Turkish citizens — a foreign-passport student at an international-track programme, for instance — are typically exempt from some or all of these MEB requirements, though the exact rule depends on the school's registration and the student's citizenship, so confirm it with the school directly rather than assuming either way.",
